    "https://www.timeshighereducation.com/unijobs/listing/416388/phd-scholarship-source-imaging-for-drug-resistant-epilepsy/": {
      "title": "PhD Scholarship, Source Imaging for Drug Resistant Epilepsy",
      "description": "PhD Scholarship, Source Imaging for Drug Resistant Epilepsy job with SWINBURNE UNIVERSITY OF TECHNOLOGY | 416388 Skip to main content News Rankings Jobs Study abroad Events Resources Solutions Skip to main menu Skip to user menu Jobseekers: Login Sign up Employers: Login (This link will open in a new window) Post a\n                          job (This link will open in a new window) Employers Jobs Home Find a job Job alerts Upload CV Careers advice the.com (This link will open in a new window) More Results PhD Scholarship, Source Imaging for Drug Resistant Epilepsy Employer SWINBURNE UNIVERSITY OF TECHNOLOGY Location Hawthorn, Australia Salary $42,440 per annum Closing date 22 Nov 2026 View more categories View less categories Academic Discipline Biological Sciences , Computer Science , Engineering & Technology , Clinical, Pre-clinical & Health , Mathematics & Statistics , Physical Sciences , Life sciences , Medicine & Dentistry , Other Health & Social Care Job Type Academic Posts , Studentships / Grants & Scholarships Contract Type Fixed Term Hours Full Time Apply on website Save job Click to add the job to your shortlist You need to sign in or create an account to save a job. Send job Job Details Help Transform the Future of Epilepsy Surgery Full-time, fixed-term (3.5 years) position at our Hawthorn campus Scholarship stipend of $42,440 per annum Are you passionate about epilepsy research, neuroimaging, and advanced data analysis? Join a cutting-edge research team at Swinburne University's Centre for Mental Health and Brain Sciences and contribute to innovative research that has the potential to improve outcomes for people living with drug-resistant epilepsy. This exciting full-time PhD scholarship is linked to a successful NHMRC Ideas Grant investigating Non-invasive Dynamic 3D Phase-Velocity Field Imaging (PVFI) to advance epilepsy surgery eligibility and outcomes. About the Project Epilepsy surgery offers hope to many patients with drug-resistant focal epilepsy, but accurately identifying the brain regions responsible for seizures remains one of the biggest challenges in clinical practice. As a PhD candidate, you will work within a multidisciplinary team to develop, validate, and translate innovative source imaging and 3D PVFI technologies using High Density Electroencephalography (HDEEG) and Magnetoencephalography (MEG). Your research will contribute to improving the localisation of epileptogenic brain regions, supporting better identification of suitable surgical candidates and ultimately enhancing surgical success rates. This project sits at the intersection of neuroscience, neuroimaging, computational modelling and clinical translation, offering a unique opportunity to undertake impactful research with real-world health outcomes. If you're looking for a PhD project that combines innovative technology, neuroscience, clinical translation and the opportunity to make a genuine difference to patients' lives, we'd love to hear from you. About You We are seeking an outstanding and highly motivated candidate with a strong academic background in a relevant discipline, including: Epilepsy research Neuroimaging Statistical analysis Computational neuroscience Biomedical engineering Computer science Medical physics Related health, science or engineering disciplines The successful applicant will be excited by the opportunity to apply advanced analytical and imaging techniques to solve complex clinical challenges and improve patient outcomes. What You'll Gain Opportunity to work on an NHMRC-funded research program with significant clinical impact Access to state-of-the-art neuroimaging technologies and research facilities Supervision and mentorship from leading researchers in neuroscience, neuroimaging and epilepsy research Experience collaborating across clinical, engineering and computational disciplines The opportunity to contribute to research that may directly influence future epilepsy surgery assessment and treatment pathways Qualifications Undergraduate degree in science, psychology, neuroimaging, computational neuroscience or related disciplines. About Swinburne University of Technology Swinburne’s strategy draws upon our understanding of future challenges. We choose to build Swinburne as the prototype of a new and different university – one that is truly of Technology, of Innovation and of Entrepreneurship. We are committed to a differentiated university proposition in education and research. Our Ad Astra strategy is the cornerstone of Swinburne’s bold ambition to lead globally in technology-driven education and research. This strategy positions us to create transformative solutions, empower learners, and partner with industry to thrive in an ever-changing world. To Apply Please submit your CV and cover letter addressing the Key Selection Criteria and your suitability for this position. To review the Position Description and to apply, please scroll down to the bottom of the page. If you are viewing this advert from an external site, please click ‘apply’ and you will be redirected to Swinburne’s Jobs website to access the Position Description at the bottom of the page. Please Note: Appointment to this position is subject to passing a Working with Children Check. If you are experiencing technical difficulties with your application, please contact the Talent Acquisition team at talentacquisition@swin.edu.au Applications Close: Sunday 22 November 2026, at 11.00pm Diversity, Equity and Inclusion Swinburne has become a world-class university, driving social and economic impacts through science, technology, and innovation. As a dual-sector university, our vision is for people and technology working together to build a better world. Central to our vision is our commitment to diversity, equity, and inclusion. We pride ourselves on being an equal opportunity employer focused on attracting, retaining, and developing great talent. We work to remove barriers related to gender identity, culture, ethnicity, sexual orientation, disability, and age. Swinburne is proud to be recognised as an AWEI Gold Employer, reflecting our commitment to creating an inclusive workplace where LGBTQIA+ employees, students and communities can thrive. We strongly encourage applicants from diverse Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander communities. Our Moondani Toombadool Centre leads our Indigenous education and culture at Swinburne, guided by community wisdom and leadership. We support applicants with disabilities, and reasonable adjustments can be requested at any stage of the recruitment process. For reasonable adjustment requests - including accessible formats of the position description, application form, or other documents, please contact talentacquisition@swin.edu.au or call +61 3 9214 3550. Please note: this phone number is for disability and reasonable adjustment enquiries only. General enquiries about the role can also be emailed to talentacquisition@swin.edu.au . Victoria’s Commitment to Action: Improving international student employment outcomes. As a signatory to Victoria’s Commitment to Action , Swinburne seeks to remove barriers to international graduate employment. We welcome and encourage applications from international graduates. Please click here for Position Description How to apply: Start your application by clicking the \"begin\" button. Login to an existing account or reset your password View our FAQ section View Hints & Tips to help with your application Preview Application Form By choosing to continue, you agree to the privacy policy. Show Privacy Policy Share this job Facebook Twitter LinkedIn Apply on website Send job Apply on website Save job Click to add the job to your shortlist You need to sign in or create an account to save a job. Get job alerts Create a job alert and receive personalised job recommendations straight to your inbox. Create alert Similar jobs Tenure-Track Professor/Associate Professor/Assistant Professor in the Department of Ophthalmology Pok Fu Lam, Hong Kong Lecturer - Healthcare Apprenticeships (Paramedic Science) Birmingham, United Kingdom PhD Scholarship in Multimodal Imaging for Epilepsy Surgery Assessment Hawthorn, Australia back to top Facebook (This link will open in a new window) Twitter (This link will open in a new window) LinkedIn (This link will open in a new window) Contact us Terms & Conditions Privacy Policy © 2015 - 2026 THE World Universities Insights Limited. Powered by Madgex Job Board Software Close Before you apply - Turn on alerts for jobs like this! We'll send them straight to your inbox : What is your email address? required When you create this job alert we will email you a selection of jobs matching your criteria. Our terms and conditions and privacy policy apply to this service and you can unsubscribe at any time. By clicking to continue to apply below, your email address will be shared with the employer. No thanks, continue to apply (This will open in a new window from which you will be automatically redirected to an external site after 5 seconds) Create alert and continue to apply (This will open in a new window from which you will be automatically redirected to an external site after 5 seconds) Madgex Design System Icon. Madgex Design System Icon."
